window.FIELDNOTES_SEARCH=[{"title":"Preparing for your first Ultraskate","section":"Choose what a good day means to you","url":"guide/start-here.html#part-1","topic":"prepare","text":"One participant used three targets: a minimum, a realistic goal and a stretch goal. Their first set was 100/120/150 miles; the next was 100/150/200 miles or staying for the full 24 hours. These are personal targets, not an entry requirement or a reason to continue when unwell."},{"title":"Preparing for your first Ultraskate","section":"Plan time off the board as well as time on it","url":"guide/start-here.html#part-2","topic":"prepare","text":"Several riders describe being overambitious about maintaining their normal riding speed for an entire day. One inline participant describes starting with a 15 mph pact and falling apart after six hours. Another first-timer returned to finish a 100-mile goal after a long overnight stop. These accounts make room for changing the plan, rather than treating a break as automatic failure."},{"title":"Preparing for your first Ultraskate","section":"Use preparation rides to answer concrete questions","url":"guide/start-here.html#part-3","topic":"prepare","text":"A first-timer’s preparation thread covers regular and mongo pushing, switch stance, foot-braking on the weaker foot and riding in different conditions. Its replies discuss reducing unnecessary foot shuffling. Treat these as skills to explore before the event, with a setup and pace you can control; the thread is not a requirement to master every technique before entering."},{"title":"Preparing for your first Ultraskate","section":"Write down your practical plan","url":"guide/start-here.html#part-4","topic":"prepare","text":"Use the packing and first-timer discussions to decide how you will get drinks and food, where your equipment will live, what happens if you need rest, and how you will leave after the event. The community accounts are most useful as prompts for your own plan, not a promise that any particular distance will follow."},{"title":"Preparing for your first Ultraskate","section":"Take the next question further","url":"guide/start-here.html#next-questions","topic":"prepare","text":"Continue with what should my training help me discover, which rules must I confirm for my edition and discipline, what should I pack, and why."},{"title":"Make training answer your race-day questions","section":"Consistency appears in very different forms","url":"guide/training-practice.html#part-1","topic":"prepare","text":"In the Dutch preparation thread, one rider describes short commuting rides several days a week plus occasional 50 km outings. Another describes easier endurance skating and leg-strength work. The goals thread includes a participant who wanted more skate-specific preparation instead of relying mainly on running and cycling. These are individual reports with different starting points, rather than a prescribed weekly schedule."},{"title":"Make training answer your race-day questions","section":"Bad weather changes the available tools","url":"guide/training-practice.html#part-2","topic":"prepare","text":"The winter discussion includes cycling, rowing, weights, cross-country skiing, indoor trainers and balance practice. It also contains disagreement about training intensity and a caution about stationary push practice loading the back differently. The useful question is which part of preparation an alternative activity serves. These experiences do not show that it replaces skating hour for hour."},{"title":"Make training answer your race-day questions","section":"Rehearse more than distance","url":"guide/training-practice.html#part-3","topic":"prepare","text":"Long rides also reveal equipment comfort, whether a drink or snack remains appealing and whether carrying a full hydration pack becomes uncomfortable. One Dutch first-timer’s detailed report links lower-back discomfort to the weight they carried and identifies chafing as a major problem. That sort of observation can be more actionable than a distance total alone."},{"title":"Make training answer your race-day questions","section":"Keep a record you can use next time","url":"guide/training-practice.html#part-4","topic":"prepare","text":"The large 2023 debrief includes a suggestion to journal the experience before the next packing cycle. A useful record separates what was planned from what happened: riding time, stops, food, gear changes and things that failed. Use this as a way to organise your own experience, rather than as a training prescription."},{"title":"Make training answer your race-day questions","section":"A daily streak records adherence, not a required programme","url":"guide/training-practice.html#streaks-and-recovery","topic":"prepare","text":"One frequent contributor progressed from day 259 of skating more than a mile daily to completing a full year of at least one mile a day. This is the continuation of one person’s habit. Their accompanying claim that it healed several health issues does not include diagnoses, measurements or an independent assessment. A useful training record can include consistency while still recording interruptions and recovery. Compare the streak with the contrasting injury accounts before treating daily riding as a rule. These experiences do not establish a minimum streak or an age-based preparation timetable."},{"title":"Make training answer your race-day questions","section":"Consistency reports are not a daily-volume prescription","url":"guide/training-practice.html#recent-r1-streak","topic":"prepare","text":"One skater reported more than a mile every day during 2025, including 20 miles each day in May, and more than 2.22 miles daily in 2026. The account did not describe recovery, injuries or a resulting Ultra performance. A separate motivational reply warned that waiting for ideal temperature, dry weather or enthusiasm could turn days without skating into weeks or months. Use these as examples of commitment, not a requirement to maintain a streak or train in unsafe conditions. Record what a session helps you practise and how you recover, alongside the mileage."},{"title":"Make training answer your race-day questions","section":"Separate planned changes from evidence that they worked","url":"guide/training-practice.html#recent-specificity-and-equipment","topic":"prepare","text":"After 167 miles at Dutch Ultra, one rider hoped to reach 200 with a proper LDP board and more skating practice, instead of relying mainly on running and cycling. They also intended to skip the 55-mile bike ride they had done two days before the previous event. A reply thought the board upgrade alone might be enough and preferred varied training with age. Neither view had a follow-up result in that discussion, so the relative effects of equipment, specific practice and pre-event activity remain unresolved."},{"title":"Make training answer your race-day questions","section":"Take the next question further","url":"guide/training-practice.html#next-questions","topic":"prepare","text":"Continue with what can I do when I cannot skate outside, what makes a route useful for Ultraskate practice, do I need to switch, and what can I practise."},{"title":"Switching feet and reducing wasted movement","section":"There is no single switching rhythm","url":"guide/push-efficiently.html#part-1","topic":"prepare","text":"The push-technique discussion contains three-to-five-push sets with occasional mongo when tired, counting in fours to reduce mental effort, and a six-part sequence alternating regular, mongo, switch and switch mongo in sets of five. The detailed five-push rider also considered changing the pattern with track sections. These are rehearsed approaches, not evidence that one count is best."},{"title":"Switching feet and reducing wasted movement","section":"Foot placement is part of the effort","url":"guide/push-efficiently.html#part-2","topic":"prepare","text":"One participant describes reducing the repeated turn of the front foot from forward-facing to sideways and back. The original questioner later reports trying that placement and noticing less extra motion. This is an exchange about what worked for them, not a universal technique standard."},{"title":"Switching feet and reducing wasted movement","section":"Pumping can be a change of movement, not just a speed target","url":"guide/push-efficiently.html#part-3","topic":"prepare","text":"A rider asking about bracket boards describes an 80% push / 20% pump pattern and using pumping to break up the pushing. Responses focus on comfort with the existing board. Another first-timer found their board too high and unstable for the event and stopped pumping early. Together these stories suggest comparing movement and comfort on your own setup before making equipment assumptions."},{"title":"Switching feet and reducing wasted movement","section":"Keep a margin for control","url":"guide/push-efficiently.html#part-4","topic":"prepare","text":"The 2023 debrief includes a wheelbite incident during enthusiastic switch pumping. It also contains concern about fast passing and pit-entry conflicts between disciplines. These are reasons to treat clearance, predictable movement and the event briefing as part of technique preparation. Follow the current event briefing for lane and passing rules."},{"title":"Switching feet and reducing wasted movement","section":"A useful cue still needs an observable result","url":"guide/push-efficiently.html#recent-r1-personal-cues","topic":"prepare","text":"A participant used lifting their knees as a reminder to make a stronger push, saying they otherwise became lazy about it. They did not report force, speed or movement measurements. It is a personal cue to examine, not a proven rule that higher knees always produce more power. When trying a cue, distinguish what you changed from what you noticed. Comfort, control and repeatability are more useful observations than assuming an energetic-looking movement must be efficient."},{"title":"Switching feet and reducing wasted movement","section":"Take the next question further","url":"guide/push-efficiently.html#next-questions","topic":"prepare","text":"Continue with do I need to switch, and what can I practise, what should I investigate before changing or repairing my setup, how do speed, stops and rest change the day."},{"title":"Choose a setup by comfort and trade-offs","section":"A bracket board is not an entry requirement","url":"guide/board-and-wheels.html#part-1","topic":"equipment","text":"The Supersonic-versus-bracket discussion begins with a rider who already likes their board. Replies include multiple 200-mile rides on a double-drop board and a Wiggler owner who values its comfort but would not justify a switch solely on performance or deck height. Another respondent advises against changing boards close to the event. Those are strong reasons to ask what problem an upgrade actually solves."},{"title":"Choose a setup by comfort and trade-offs","section":"Wheel discussions are about competing priorities","url":"guide/board-and-wheels.html#part-2","topic":"equipment","text":"The Miami wheel thread repeatedly contrasts rough corners with smoother straights. Riders disagree about wheel feel, weight, grip and comfort, and some bring more than one set. Comments about particular urethane formulas and models are historical personal comparisons; the discussion does not produce a controlled ranking or a reliable replacement interval."},{"title":"Choose a setup by comfort and trade-offs","section":"Do not confuse a confident claim with a measured gain","url":"guide/board-and-wheels.html#part-3","topic":"equipment","text":"In the mistakes thread, one rider wonders whether different bearings would have saved effort; another points out diminishing returns and says the bearings already used were decent. These are different opinions, rather than a measured comparison. It does not establish a distance gain from buying more expensive bearings."},{"title":"Choose a setup by comfort and trade-offs","section":"Validate the whole setup","url":"guide/board-and-wheels.html#part-4","topic":"equipment","text":"The wheelbite account in the 2023 debrief is a useful counterweight to brand enthusiasm: a setup can feel excellent and still have a clearance problem under a particular movement. Packing discussions also mention spares and an alternative board, especially when a mechanical problem would otherwise end the day."},{"title":"Choose a setup by comfort and trade-offs","section":"Ball count did not settle the bearing question","url":"guide/board-and-wheels.html#recent-r1-bearing-count","topic":"equipment","text":"A six-versus-seven-ball discussion offered the claim that fewer balls mean less friction through less contact, plus an ambiguous reply about acceleration. It contained no matched comparison or measured result, and the original poster jokingly concluded only that wheels need bearings. The discussion does not establish ball count as a performance ranking."},{"title":"Choose a setup by comfort and trade-offs","section":"A cheap conversion price may cover only part of the board","url":"guide/board-and-wheels.html#recent-r1-conversion-cost","topic":"equipment","text":"A January 2026 post proposed roughly $110 in G-Bomb parts plus a cut-down used or broken deck as an inexpensive way into a bracket/torsion setup. Another rider liked a similar arrangement with a DT Poppy front. The figure was a historical parts estimate, not the total cost of a complete tested board, and the discussion did not establish the structural suitability of a damaged deck. Separate the price of the named parts from the complete build, compatibility checks and a sound deck. A photo and an enthusiastic reply are not instructions for cutting or reusing damaged equipment."},{"title":"Choose a setup by comfort and trade-offs","section":"Take the next question further","url":"guide/board-and-wheels.html#next-questions","topic":"equipment","text":"Continue with what should I investigate before changing or repairing my setup, which shoe and clothing trade-offs matter after many hours, what should I pack, and why."},{"title":"Shoes, grip and comfort over many hours","section":"Read both sides of the barefoot-shoe discussion","url":"guide/shoes-and-comfort.html#part-1","topic":"equipment","text":"A primarily paddling push/paddle rider with wide feet liked Xero shoes used in training and at the previous Ultra. Another reported a 156-mile personal best in Merrell Vapor Glove 3 shoes but considered the soles too thin to use again. A third credited different Merrell barefoot models with resolving their earlier discomfort, after an adaptation period. A fourth changed from minimalist shoes to traditional skate shoes at mile 80—and changed to a lower board at the same time—then enjoyed the final 20 miles. Because two things changed together, that account cannot isolate the shoes as the cause. Compare fit and comfort over long rides before choosing a shoe category."},{"title":"Shoes, grip and comfort over many hours","section":"Wear depends on what you do with the shoes","url":"guide/shoes-and-comfort.html#part-2","topic":"equipment","text":"The Speedforce wear discussion compares substantial sole wear after roughly 600 miles with other users reporting much longer service. The follow-up identifies foot-braking, model differences and rubber grip as possible differences between experiences. These anecdotes cannot establish a universal lifespan, and old warranty comments are not current warranty terms."},{"title":"Shoes, grip and comfort over many hours","section":"Small discomforts can become the limiting factor","url":"guide/shoes-and-comfort.html#part-3","topic":"equipment","text":"Riders name chafing, blisters, hot feet and foot numbness among the things that disrupted otherwise workable plans. Packing discussions mention spare shoes, dry layers and anti-chafe supplies. These are reports about preparation and comfort, not diagnoses or treatment recommendations."},{"title":"Shoes, grip and comfort over many hours","section":"Clothing has practical consequences","url":"guide/shoes-and-comfort.html#part-4","topic":"equipment","text":"A rider’s mistakes list includes a large puffy jacket that felt slow in wind, and several shoe changes that helped them continue. That account puts layers, weather and foot comfort alongside board choice. Use it to build your own tested options rather than copying a full kit list uncritically."},{"title":"Shoes, grip and comfort over many hours","section":"A familiar size may not mean a familiar fit","url":"guide/shoes-and-comfort.html#recent-fit-and-adaptation","topic":"equipment","text":"The rider who preferred Merrell barefoot shoes described needing days or weeks to adapt to the thin soles. Their usual European size 42 became 43.5 in those shoes. That is one person’s fit report, not a conversion chart or an adaptation timetable for someone else. It adds a practical detail to the conflicting minimalist-shoe experiences above: compare the actual fit, not just the size printed on the box."},{"title":"Shoes, grip and comfort over many hours","section":"Take the next question further","url":"guide/shoes-and-comfort.html#next-questions","topic":"equipment","text":"Continue with what explains conflicting comfort reports, what problem would a different setup actually solve, when should community advice give way to medical help."},{"title":"Food and drinks: learn from the disagreements","section":"Liquid versus solid is an unresolved preference","url":"guide/food-and-drinks.html#part-1","topic":"race","text":"The pizza thread has riders describing a bad experience with too much Tailwind, others reporting good results on mostly liquid calories, and others preferring regular meals and snacks. No single menu emerges as a consensus. The recurring practical theme is to know how your chosen foods and drinks behave for you before event day."},{"title":"Food and drinks: learn from the disagreements","section":"Variety is also about willingness to keep eating","url":"guide/food-and-drinks.html#part-2","topic":"race","text":"In the Dutch preparation and vegetarian discussions, contributors mention flavour fatigue and wanting something salty or substantial after hours of sweet drinks. Examples include potatoes, rice, bananas, applesauce pouches, crisps and familiar snacks. These are options people reported using, not a nutritionally complete menu or evidence that a food will suit everyone."},{"title":"Food and drinks: learn from the disagreements","section":"Vegetarian and vegan accounts are not the same as one another","url":"guide/food-and-drinks.html#part-3","topic":"race","text":"The vegetarian/vegan thread includes a long-term vegan participant who uses drink powders and snacks, a lifelong vegetarian who describes provided meals and fruit pouches, and others who mix ordinary foods with gels. Some examples elsewhere in the thread contain honey or dairy. Check the original contributor’s approach and the actual ingredients rather than treating every suggestion in that thread as vegan."},{"title":"Food and drinks: learn from the disagreements","section":"Keep medical and numerical claims in context","url":"guide/food-and-drinks.html#part-4","topic":"race","text":"Community advice sometimes includes personal experiences with diabetes, insulin management, caffeine and intake targets. An individual health plan needs advice suited to that person. See the boundary between experience and medical guidance before interpreting symptoms or medication advice."},{"title":"Food and drinks: learn from the disagreements","section":"Resupply belongs in the food plan","url":"guide/food-and-drinks.html#part-5","topic":"race","text":"One solo rider prepared 12–14 mixed bottles in rows by flavour, with caffeinated and non-caffeinated options plus plain water. They used about 8–10 because they also ate meals. Another shared a race-map spreadsheet but explicitly said the race did not follow it exactly. These accounts concern organisation, not an hourly intake prescription."},{"title":"Food and drinks: learn from the disagreements","section":"Frequent fuel and proper meals can coexist","url":"guide/food-and-drinks.html#meals-and-frequency","topic":"race","text":"A later discussion opens with a preference for small, regular amounts and avoiding heavy meals. A reply describes a different successful arrangement: cereal and banana before the start, gels roughly every thirty minutes when remembered, catered lunch and dinner, then turkey sandwiches at midnight and breakfast. It was that rider’s first edition spent riding throughout the 24 hours. The account challenges a blanket rule to avoid meals; it does not establish a gel schedule for everyone. Another contributor preferred ordinary food to gels in both skating and cycling: sandwiches, canned food and fruit that could be eaten while moving. Strong flavours helped keep eating interesting, and they accepted a slower lap to eat rather than stopping. A 210-mile finisher instead alternated water with carbohydrate drink mixes for most calories, using solids mainly for morale. They kept one familiar snack they could usually face even when nauseated—Slim Jims in their case. Their claimed 16,000-kcal deficit has no calculation or measurement method in the thread and cannot be treated as an energy requirement. The useful comparison is timing, portion size, portability and what remains appealing late in the day. The same discussion’s explanations of digestion and claims about pickles preventing cramps are not established by these experiences. Keep symptoms and treatment questions with the medical guidance, rather than treating a successful menu as proof of its proposed mechanism."},{"title":"Food and drinks: learn from the disagreements","section":"The same dinner helped one rider and troubled another","url":"guide/food-and-drinks.html#recent-r1-meal-conflict","topic":"race","text":"A January 2026 discussion began with a rider saying that staying on the board was easier than staying fuelled. Another described nausea all afternoon and into the evening at their first Ultra. The thread then split over arroz con pollo: one rider advised avoiding it, while another said their nausea eased after eating it. The second rider explicitly wondered whether the cooler evening, rather than the food, explained the improvement. Elsewhere in the same discussion, a rider feeling they were crashing reported renewed energy after ordering cheese pizza and went on to their first podium. That is an individual sequence of events, not evidence that pizza produces a performance gain. Together, these accounts support keeping accepted alternatives available and recording timing, heat and appetite alongside the food itself."},{"title":"Food and drinks: learn from the disagreements","section":"A dietary selection is still a detail to confirm","url":"guide/food-and-drinks.html#recent-r1-provided-meals","topic":"race","text":"One participant said they found little or no vegan provision in the previous year despite selecting vegan, and did not want the meals they had paid for. They also reported that the next entry bundled meals into the price instead of offering them separately. This is one historical complaint, not a statement about the current menu or registration terms. Confirm ingredients, serving times and the actual dietary option for your edition, then retain familiar food you can use if the provided meal does not work for you. The fact that a meal is included does not answer whether it fits your diet or remains appealing during the race."},{"title":"Food and drinks: learn from the disagreements","section":"Food anecdotes do not identify a cramp remedy","url":"guide/food-and-drinks.html#recent-r1-cramp-stories","topic":"race","text":"A rider credited bananas with avoiding cramps. Another reported cramping at 60 miles in an earlier Ultra, then eating dill pickles and drinking their juice, having no further cramps and reaching 154 miles. The account gives no quantities or full timeline and does not isolate food from changes in pace, rest, weather or other intake. Neither story establishes a preventive treatment. Keep a food you enjoy separate from a claim that it treats a symptom. Use the health guidance when deciding how to respond to concerning symptoms."},{"title":"Food and drinks: learn from the disagreements","section":"Use the menu examples with their original context","url":"guide/food-and-drinks.html#recent-plant-based-examples","topic":"race","text":"The lifelong vegetarian inline skater was aiming to stay out as long as possible, rather than race for a placing. Their examples included 70–80-calorie puréed food pouches, bananas, concentrated sports drink and homemade bars, with honey sweets and chocolate-covered almonds or coffee beans for enjoyment. A participant reporting nine vegan Ultras instead described relying heavily on Skratch and Tailwind alongside applesauce pouches, pickles, chips and other snacks, with occasional protein shakes. Honey-containing examples are not vegan; neither account is a complete menu or an intake target. One rider disliked gels because they were sticky and created rubbish. They planned to try high-carbohydrate powders and hot rice packets around 2 am, while another found cucumber and carrot snacks comfortable to eat. The rice-and-powder plan was prospective, not a successful event report. If a familiar food needs heat or refrigeration, connect that choice to your confirmed cooking and storage arrangements. The thread also disagrees about restrictions. One powder-and-gel user proposed avoiding anything above 8 g of protein, while others described protein shakes, bars, nuts or avocado. The participants gave no evidence for a universal 8 g cutoff. Preserve that disagreement instead of turning one person’s preference into a rule for every rider."},{"title":"Food and drinks: learn from the disagreements","section":"Plan for a different appetite late in the event","url":"guide/food-and-drinks.html#recent-changing-appetite","topic":"race","text":"A mixed-food participant described switching to liquid Tailwind for roughly the final third when eating became difficult. The questioner had the opposite preference for something that felt solid and found repeated Maurten gels excessively sweet. Another participant wanted fresh or savory snacks after hours of sweet drink and felt that eating at midnight provided reassurance as well as energy. They described their effort as moderate and explicitly thought the experience might differ for riders exceeding 250 miles with less time to stop. Check what product you actually tested. When the questioner described Tailwind as chalky, a reply asked whether it was the recovery version rather than the endurance mix. The product in the bag was never confirmed. The useful next step is to check the label and preparation instructions, not assume that everyone using the same brand has tried the same drink. Overnight caffeine preferences also varied: one participant used cold coffee intermittently, while another delayed caffeine and later used caffeinated gels or drink mix. Neither gave a dose or evidence of sustained alertness. These are taste and timing accounts, not a substitute for the rest needed for a safe journey home."},{"title":"Food and drinks: learn from the disagreements","section":"Take the next question further","url":"guide/food-and-drinks.html#next-questions","topic":"race","text":"Continue with how can I organise supplies without a full-time crew, what does a support person need to do, when should community advice give way to medical help."},{"title":"Pace the whole event, including the stops","section":"Moving speed is not overall average speed","url":"guide/pace-and-rest.html#part-1","topic":"race","text":"The goals discussion includes an ambitious 14 mph expectation followed by a 113-mile result, and an inline rider’s account of a fast early pact that unravelled. Both illustrate the difference between a speed that feels possible early and the distance achieved across the entire event. The calculator on this site makes the arithmetic explicit; it does not predict what your body can sustain."},{"title":"Pace the whole event, including the stops","section":"Continuous riding and planned breaks","url":"guide/pace-and-rest.html#part-2","topic":"race","text":"One Dutch participant described riding blocks separated by 30-minute stops. A support person reported a 250-mile plan split into five 50-mile blocks, with three one-hour breaks and a two-hour night break. Another rider found restarting after stopping difficult. These approaches serve different people and goals; they do not establish an optimal stop schedule. Compare these approaches with your goals and how you feel after restarting."},{"title":"Pace the whole event, including the stops","section":"A lower target can still be a successful first event","url":"guide/pace-and-rest.html#part-3","topic":"race","text":"The first-timer discussion follows someone aiming for 100 miles, taking hours off overnight after food problems, and eventually reaching the target. Other goal-setting replies explicitly use three possible outcomes. This leaves room for adaptation without presenting persistence through illness or injury as a rule."},{"title":"Pace the whole event, including the stops","section":"Rest needs a place and a plan","url":"guide/pace-and-rest.html#part-4","topic":"race","text":"Pit-crew and family-logistics discussions describe lights, noise and difficulty sleeping at the venue. Accounts disagree about music schedules. Their common planning value is that a tent alone is not a guarantee of rest; contributors discuss eye masks, ear protection, sleeping surfaces and accommodation. Confirm what the current venue actually permits and provides."},{"title":"Pace the whole event, including the stops","section":"The same venue produced different sleep reports","url":"guide/pace-and-rest.html#recent-r1-noise","topic":"race","text":"In one family discussion, a participant remembered the garages becoming quiet after music stopped after dark. Another remembered music throughout the race and said the nominal 10 pm quiet period before race day did not reliably mean quiet. Other replies singled out garage snoring. A later supporter thread variously recalled no night music, a stop around midnight, or music late into the night and early again. These accounts cannot establish a dependable quiet window. Light was another problem: participants reported pit or parking lights staying on both the night before and race night. Their sleep arrangements included eye masks, earplugs with additional over-ear protection, a cot without a tent, and a camping mat or air mattress in a vehicle with flat seats. One camper preferred a hotel after finding track sleep difficult. A vehicle’s ability to block sound does not establish a safe temperature inside it. A separate suggestion was a hammock between concrete pillars; permission and suitable attachment points were not established. The useful requirement is a rest arrangement you can actually use, including a way to lie down, rather than assuming any available chair or tent will provide sleep."},{"title":"Pace the whole event, including the stops","section":"Distinguish a paper schedule from a demonstrated result","url":"guide/pace-and-rest.html#recent-targets-and-early-pace","topic":"race","text":"The rider who finished 113 miles after expecting 14 mph for 24 hours returned with 125 miles as a first target and 150 if time allowed. They thought 200 might work as four 50-mile blocks with one-hour rests, but explicitly left feasibility to the day itself. That was a proposed schedule, unlike the completed block-based accounts above. The 15 mph inline account also contains an early warning: the rider noticed an unexpectedly high heart-rate response on the first lap and found the track surface unlike the Miami asphalt they knew. The group nevertheless held the pace for six hours before struggling; that rider quit below 150 miles, while one companion slowed to about 12 mph and exceeded 300. The different outcomes within one group are another reason not to borrow its opening speed as a target."},{"title":"Pace the whole event, including the stops","section":"Take the next question further","url":"guide/pace-and-rest.html#next-questions","topic":"race","text":"Continue with what helped people through the mental part of the event, how can I avoid misreading or losing my ride data, when should community advice give way to medical help."},{"title":"Build a pit plan your support person can use","section":"Give support a job description","url":"guide/pit-crew.html#part-1","topic":"logistics","text":"In a parent’s first-time support thread, experienced participants describe preparing bottles, mixing supplies and handing over food and drinks. One contributor recommends practising handoffs; another mentions a small sling bag for supplies. These accounts turn ‘being there to help’ into concrete tasks. Current event rules still determine where handoffs can happen."},{"title":"Build a pit plan your support person can use","section":"Separate the rider’s needs from the crew’s","url":"guide/pit-crew.html#part-2","topic":"logistics","text":"Replies remind the support person to bring their own food, drinks, seating, layers and somewhere to rest. Family-logistics contributors describe how long and tiring the event can be for non-riders too. A comfortable crew base is part of the plan, not an afterthought once the rider is ready."},{"title":"Build a pit plan your support person can use","section":"A solo plan can be organised too","url":"guide/pit-crew.html#part-3","topic":"logistics","text":"A solo-rider account describes arranging prepared bottles by flavour and keeping plain water accessible. This is an example of reducing the decisions needed during the ride. The exact location, container rules and access arrangements need confirmation with the organiser for the relevant edition."},{"title":"Build a pit plan your support person can use","section":"Treat venue details as questions to confirm","url":"guide/pit-crew.html#part-4","topic":"logistics","text":"The support thread contains different descriptions of parking near pits, access to electricity, packet pickup and overnight music. Some clarify that the parking lot is not the pit row itself. Rather than blending those statements into one authoritative description, use them as a list of questions for the current organiser."},{"title":"Build a pit plan your support person can use","section":"Separate parking, pit space and shared facilities","url":"guide/pit-crew.html#recent-r1-pit-access","topic":"logistics","text":"Replies to a first-time supporter initially described parking as being in the pit. Another clarified that cars were in a lot starting a few hundred feet from pit row; a third recalled spaces reserved for camping the previous night. One thought arriving earlier could shorten the walk to the garage. Ask about the exact place for the car, the equipment and the handoff separately, rather than assuming the word “pit” describes all three. Power was reported along the wall between the track and pit, with limited outlets and a need to share with neighbours. That does not establish available electrical load or permission for a cooking appliance. At Dutch Ultra, a participant described tents and party tents holding food, drink and equipment; no pitch allocation or dimensions were given. A Miami attendee recalled refill water being supplied in recent years and discussed both bottle swaps and hydration packs. Confirm the refill point and how you will reach it before deciding what water to transport. Their estimate of daytime drinking was personal planning advice, not an individual fluid target; see the limits of copied hydration advice."},{"title":"Build a pit plan your support person can use","section":"Rehearse requests as well as the handoff","url":"guide/pit-crew.html#recent-r1-rehearsal","topic":"logistics","text":"One experienced attendee valued practice handoffs because the pit straight was smooth, often had a tailwind and followed a corner exit that carried speed. A forced stop there cost momentum. Those are historical observations, not a reason to rush a handoff or assume the same wind, surface or traffic next time. A reply proposed small walkie-talkies so a rider could request food, drink or equipment before arriving, explicitly recommending testing them before the event. Another rider prepared fuel the night before and handed it over in a small shoulder sling bag. The bag’s return also needs an agreed procedure; their report of tossing it over a wall is not a general handoff instruction. Rehearse the request, preparation, transfer and return with the equipment you intend to use. Include a way to stop safely when the transfer is missed, and follow the event’s permitted assistance area."},{"title":"Build a pit plan your support person can use","section":"Take the next question further","url":"guide/pit-crew.html#next-questions","topic":"logistics","text":"Continue with how can I organise supplies without a full-time crew, what should I pack, and why, which rules must I confirm for my edition and discipline."},{"title":"A packing checklist with reasons behind it","section":"Board and equipment","url":"guide/packing.html#part-1","topic":"logistics","text":"The packing thread names the board and helmet as the starting point, then adds spares and extra gear in case of failure. The support discussion also mentions a spare board where available. This is a prompt to bring compatible tools and parts, not a requirement to buy a second complete setup."},{"title":"A packing checklist with reasons behind it","section":"Conditions and comfort","url":"guide/packing.html#part-2","topic":"logistics","text":"Contributors list spare shoes, dry and warm clothes, a headlight, sunglasses, sunscreen and a power bank. A tent is discussed both as a sleeping place and a way to keep equipment dry. Night riding at the Dutch event adds a specific suggestion: clear glasses for insects, alongside daytime eyewear."},{"title":"A packing checklist with reasons behind it","section":"Food access and the small things","url":"guide/packing.html#part-3","topic":"logistics","text":"Riders describe jersey pockets, hydration bags, prepared bottles and food variety. One packing reply adds a toothbrush because hours of sweet foods and drinks can become unpleasant. These details illustrate why practising access and comfort matters as much as putting enough items in a bag."},{"title":"A packing checklist with reasons behind it","section":"Sleep and after the finish","url":"guide/packing.html#part-4","topic":"logistics","text":"The support thread includes eye masks, earplugs, chairs and sleeping pads. The 2023 debrief repeatedly raises unsafe post-event driving. Make the arrangements for leaving and resting before the event, rather than leaving that decision until you are exhausted."},{"title":"A packing checklist with reasons behind it","section":"Equipment for people who are waiting","url":"guide/packing.html#recent-r1-family-equipment","topic":"logistics","text":"For teenagers staying trackside, one parent recommended canopy shelter, chairs, blankets, books, phone batteries and extra snacks. They had given bulky purchased equipment to other attendees afterward. Another member offered a loan through a friend on a pay-it-forward basis. These show possible ways to handle overseas luggage limits; they do not establish an equipment rental or lending service. A supporter also pointed out that sitting through sun and a cool night creates different needs from skating. Their list included their own food, iced cooler, drinks, seating and layers, plus clean clothes, wipes and a towel for after the event. Arrange the crew’s supplies as deliberately as the rider’s."},{"title":"A packing checklist with reasons behind it","section":"Take the next question further","url":"guide/packing.html#next-questions","topic":"logistics","text":"Continue with how can I organise supplies without a full-time crew, what explains conflicting comfort reports, how do I organise travel, family and the journey home."},{"title":"Travel, family and accommodation: what to ask","section":"Keep travel days and event days distinct","url":"guide/travel-logistics.html#part-1","topic":"logistics","text":"In the family-logistics thread, contributors discuss arriving in time for shopping, supplies and accommodation before the event. They distinguish staying at a hotel from setting up at the track. These are different comfort and luggage trade-offs, especially for someone travelling internationally."},{"title":"Travel, family and accommodation: what to ask","section":"Supporters need their own realistic day","url":"guide/travel-logistics.html#part-2","topic":"logistics","text":"A parent travelling with teenagers receives advice about supervision, boredom, meals, shelter and the possibility of a shorter riding goal. Replies distinguish crew registration from volunteering, but those details are edition-specific. The durable lesson is to plan the family’s experience explicitly rather than assuming they can simply wait trackside for 24 hours."},{"title":"Travel, family and accommodation: what to ask","section":"Flying with a board has several historical solutions","url":"guide/travel-logistics.html#part-3","topic":"logistics","text":"The board-travel thread includes removing brackets and packing components and taking a complete board into the cabin. It also contains a reported exception where an airline handled the board separately. These experiences are not proof of what a particular airline will allow today; confirm dimensions, baggage rules and protection before booking or packing."},{"title":"Travel, family and accommodation: what to ask","section":"Book the recovery part of the trip","url":"guide/travel-logistics.html#part-4","topic":"logistics","text":"The 2023 debrief and family thread include serious accounts of sleepiness while driving after the event. Contributors discuss keeping a hotel room longer or arranging a place to rest. Check current availability and prices before relying on a past hotel or park recommendation."},{"title":"Travel, family and accommodation: what to ask","section":"Coordinate accommodation and transport together","url":"guide/travel-logistics.html#shared-room-and-transfer","topic":"logistics","text":"One group offered a spare room with two beds and its own bathroom in a shared rental, alongside space in a twelve-passenger van. The arrangement shows how a room offer can also solve a transfer problem; it is a historical example, not an available booking or guaranteed ride. Agree dates, seats, luggage space and the return trip explicitly. Before adding sightseeing or training outings, read how far Miami destinations were from riders’ accommodation."},{"title":"Travel, family and accommodation: what to ask","section":"Plan supervision across the whole visit","url":"guide/travel-logistics.html#recent-r1-family-hours","topic":"logistics","text":"A parent flying from the UK with two teenagers could not settle the booking until they understood who would supervise the children while they skated. Replies described families attending and crew entries including meals, but nobody confirmed a childcare service or an assigned supervisor. Crew registration and event volunteering were described as separate roles; a possible volunteer fee waiver was only a respondent’s recollection. One family’s tolerance for sitting trackside was about six hours. The same contributor illustrated how a race morning beginning around 6 am could stretch through awards around 10 am the next day. Their alternative was a personal 100-mile goal over roughly 12–14 hours, a hotel night and a return for the finish. Those times describe a suggested historical plan, not the current timetable or a pace promise. Make a separate plan for supervision, meals, entertainment, sleep and transport when the family wants to leave. Agree who can carry out that plan while you are still riding."},{"title":"Travel, family and accommodation: what to ask","section":"Check when the recovery room stops being yours","url":"guide/travel-logistics.html#recent-r1-checkout","topic":"logistics","text":"A rider travelling without their usual driver considered paying for an extra hotel day so they could sleep after the race beyond the normal noon checkout. Their previous experience of finishing sore and severely sleep deprived made the room’s departure time a practical constraint, not just its distance from the track. Put the checkout time, rested driver and onward journey on the same itinerary. A room booked only for the night before the finish may not provide the recovery time you intended. See the guidance on drowsy driving."},{"title":"Travel, family and accommodation: what to ask","section":"Dutch accommodation: route, pets and early breakfast","url":"guide/travel-logistics.html#recent-r1-dutch-stay","topic":"logistics","text":"Dutch Ultra visitors compared EuroParcs accommodation with a hotel near the track. One noted that a site was across the canal and chose a similarly priced hotel for comfort; another chose EuroParcs because they were bringing dogs. A guest at Spaarnwoude Park Hotel reported an option for Saturday breakfast boxes, while another questioned its dog policy. These are 2026 booking experiences, with no confirmed present prices, pet rules or breakfast service. A different group planned to arrive a week early and stay at EuroParcs Buitenhuizen from Thursday to Monday, mixing skating with city and nature visits. Their question about relying on public transport and boards instead of renting a car remained unanswered. The discussion also distinguishes EuroParcs Buitenhuizen from EuroParcs Spaarnwoude: the exact property matters when working out access. Check the actual journey to the entrance, the earliest breakfast you can obtain, luggage transport and any pet requirements before choosing accommodation. Keep sightseeing distance separate from the skating you want to arrive rested enough to do."},{"title":"Travel, family and accommodation: what to ask","section":"Plan for the packed board and the possibility of delayed luggage","url":"guide/travel-logistics.html#recent-packed-board-and-tools","topic":"logistics","text":"A December 2025 exchange made the deck difference concrete. A bracket-board rider could remove the brackets for a carry-on bag; a Supersonic rider used a purpose-built checked suitcase because the deck could not be shortened. Another rider’s reason for preferring carry-on was the risk of reaching Miami without their familiar setup if checked luggage went missing. These are packing choices, not guarantees of airline acceptance. A Bandito owner described a 23-inch deck against a quoted 22-inch carry-on limit, and someone proposed packing it diagonally. Neither the airline limit nor the finished bag dimensions were verified in the discussion. A diagonal fit inside a bag does not by itself establish that the packed bag meets the applicable limit. Tools need a separate check. One contributor had heard of skate tools being challenged or confiscated in carry-on baggage and suggested using checked luggage when available. That is a secondhand experience, not a security rule. Confirm your carrier’s baggage dimensions and the relevant security authority’s tool restrictions before deciding what goes in each bag."},{"title":"Travel, family and accommodation: what to ask","section":"Take the next question further","url":"guide/travel-logistics.html#next-questions","topic":"logistics","text":"Continue with which rules must I confirm for my edition and discipline, when should community advice give way to medical help, what does a support person need to do."},{"title":"Prepare for darkness, weather and changing surfaces","section":"Lighting at one event is not a guarantee for the next","url":"guide/night-and-weather.html#part-1","topic":"race","text":"The 2023 debrief includes a report of failed lights near turn four and a contributor’s plan to bring a headlamp next time. The Dutch discussion weighs headlamp brightness against runtime and mentions helmet-strap grip. Use those accounts to test your lighting arrangement rather than relying on a historical model’s advertised figures."},{"title":"Prepare for darkness, weather and changing surfaces","section":"Night preparation includes visibility and comfort","url":"guide/night-and-weather.html#part-2","topic":"race","text":"A Dutch contributor recommends clear glasses to keep insects away from the eyes when riding with a headlamp, and describes using different eyewear by day and night. Another mentions photochromic glasses. These equipment experiences do not establish that one lens or product suits every condition."},{"title":"Prepare for darkness, weather and changing surfaces","section":"Weather can change what felt manageable in training","url":"guide/night-and-weather.html#part-3","topic":"race","text":"The mistakes thread includes a rider surprised by the temperature difference between home and Florida. The 2023 debrief has a similar account from a Pacific Northwest rider. These are reminders to consider the actual event environment alongside the distances you train, without turning a personal cooling or drinking practice into universal advice."},{"title":"Prepare for darkness, weather and changing surfaces","section":"Course texture matters over time","url":"guide/night-and-weather.html#part-4","topic":"race","text":"The Miami wheel discussion describes chattery corners and smoother straights, with disagreements over wheel choice. The debrief also mentions difficult transitions and visibility around merging areas. Conditions, layout and resurfacing can change: read the historical experience, then confirm the current course and briefing."},{"title":"Prepare for darkness, weather and changing surfaces","section":"Generally lit does not mean a personal light is redundant","url":"guide/night-and-weather.html#lighting-outage-disagreement","topic":"race","text":"A visitor who forgot their light and batteries was reassured that Miami was usually well lit. One returning rider could see dropped objects and surface changes under the track lights; another described darker turns but manageable visibility without a personal light. These were reports from particular visits. The replies then disagreed about outages. One contributor described late-night switch-offs and delays restoring light as routine. An eight-edition participant said a failure happened the previous year but not at their other seven editions. These reports give a reason to prepare for an outage, without establishing that lights are routinely switched off every night. In a separate training thread, a rider regretted going out at night without a headlamp or board light and planned both next time. Another mentioned using Velcro to mount lights. The incident itself is not described, and the mounting suggestion has no retention test. Check visibility, battery life and secure attachment on your own setup."},{"title":"Prepare for darkness, weather and changing surfaces","section":"Arrival conditions may not be race conditions","url":"guide/night-and-weather.html#arrival-and-race-weather","topic":"race","text":"A UK visitor found Miami so hot on arrival that they considered riding mainly at night. Replies expected a colder race week, while the visitor’s preparation had been in rain and fog. A Chicago rider wondered whether temperatures around 40°F would make the trip feel unexpectedly familiar. These are changing expectations, not a verified weather record. Another pre-race discussion about a 13-mph north-wind forecast focused on an uphill first turn. One rider proposed using mongo to spare their normal push; another preferred this to the west winds they remembered on the backstretch. There is no follow-up showing that the proposed tactic worked. Wind direction and the part of the course matter alongside its reported speed."},{"title":"Prepare for darkness, weather and changing surfaces","section":"Florida preparation can include cold outings too","url":"guide/night-and-weather.html#recent-r1-florida-cold","topic":"race","text":"Two January 2026 comments described a ride at Santos Trailhead in Ocala as very cold. Neither described the temperature or clothing worn. Alongside the hotter-event accounts above, this is a reminder to plan for the actual location and time of day; a Florida destination alone does not describe the conditions of every practice session."},{"title":"Prepare for darkness, weather and changing surfaces","section":"The outing includes the journey home","url":"guide/night-and-weather.html#recent-journey-home-weather","topic":"race","text":"One rider accepted cutting a planned distance or duration short when dark storm clouds approached because the ride home would be on a motorcycle. The decision considered the whole outing, not just conditions while skating. This is a personal judgment, not a weather assessment or a storm-safety protocol. When planning a practice session, include the time and conditions needed to get home. Completing the planned mileage is only one part of that decision."},{"title":"Prepare for darkness, weather and changing surfaces","section":"Take the next question further","url":"guide/night-and-weather.html#next-questions","topic":"race","text":"Continue with what explains conflicting comfort reports, when should community advice give way to medical help, what should I pack, and why."},{"title":"Turn a race report into a better next attempt","section":"Separate the outcome from the reasons","url":"guide/debrief-and-return.html#part-1","topic":"after","text":"The 2023 debrief brings together food experiences, heat, equipment, passing conflicts, rest and getting home. It shows why a distance alone cannot explain how an event went. A useful personal debrief records the conditions and the decisions that changed the day, including what worked well."},{"title":"Turn a race report into a better next attempt","section":"Keep contradictory experiences visible","url":"guide/debrief-and-return.html#part-2","topic":"after","text":"Some participants describe the provided dinner as too heavy; another says the solid meal carried them through the night. Others differ about when to stop, what shoes to wear and how much nutrition to carry. A useful knowledgebank preserves these differences with attribution instead of averaging them into advice nobody actually gave."},{"title":"Turn a race report into a better next attempt","section":"Include safety and the journey home","url":"guide/debrief-and-return.html#part-3","topic":"after","text":"Repeated accounts in the debrief describe near-sleep or difficulty driving after the event. Riders also raise unclear pit entry, fast passing and difficulty identifying first-aid provision. These belong in the planning questions for the next edition. The source comments are historical reports, not current statements about the organiser’s facilities or rules."},{"title":"Turn a race report into a better next attempt","section":"Write the notes while you remember","url":"guide/debrief-and-return.html#part-4","topic":"after","text":"One contributor explicitly suggests keeping a journal to read before packing for the next event. A useful structure is: keep, change, test and confirm. Keep what worked; change one clearly identified problem; test uncertain gear or food before relying on it; confirm event-specific arrangements with the organiser."},{"title":"Turn a race report into a better next attempt","section":"The same distance can be a different outcome","url":"guide/debrief-and-return.html#recent-same-distance-different-finish","topic":"after","text":"One inline rider described stopping at 150 miles in 2023, reaching 250 in 2024 but being too uncomfortable to enjoy the awards, then reaching 250 again in 2025 and walking onto the stage. They did not explain enough changes to establish why recovery differed. The account still adds a useful debrief question: how did you finish, and what could you comfortably do afterward, alongside the distance total?"},{"title":"Turn a race report into a better next attempt","section":"Take the next question further","url":"guide/debrief-and-return.html#next-questions","topic":"after","text":"Continue with when should community advice give way to medical help, how can I avoid misreading or losing my ride data, what should my training help me discover."},{"title":"Winter training and the limits of transfer","section":"What people actually did","url":"guide/winter-and-cross-training.html#alternatives","topic":"prepare","text":"A cold-weather alternative reported in New Jersey was 10 minutes on an elliptical, stretching, 10 minutes brisk treadmill walking and another roughly 10 minutes stretching. The rider disliked gym sessions but used one when outdoor conditions were cold. Not a sufficient training prescription or measured transfer to skating. Winter alternatives included cycling for one hour at high heart rate and two-hour lower-intensity elliptical sessions every other day; the questioner later disclosed a knee injury and need for low-impact activity. These are examples of sessions, not prescribed intensity or duration. Keep fitness work separate from proving that feet, equipment and supplies tolerate skating."},{"title":"Winter training and the limits of transfer","section":"Injury, weather and crowded indoor space","url":"guide/winter-and-cross-training.html#constraints","topic":"prepare","text":"One rider preparing after a calf strain planned a cycling century first and then a gradual rebuild of skating volume; another used running on a cold morning. No rehabilitation recommendation or proof of equivalent skate fitness. Unable to skate outdoors in winter, two riders used a local rink but found it crowded on New Year’s Day, completing 191 loops in 1 hour 56 minutes while navigating other users. No lap length; cannot convert to mileage or imply rink matches Ultra surface/traffic. A rink may offer movement when roads are unusable, while adding its own traffic and turning constraints. Record the setting when comparing sessions."},{"title":"Winter training and the limits of transfer","section":"How well does other training transfer?","url":"guide/winter-and-cross-training.html#transfer","topic":"prepare","text":"After roughly nine miles mainly on a pump track, a rider explicitly wondered whether it helped or hindered Ultra preparation; no response resolving that transfer question is available. Paddling was still unavailable after the earlier shoulder problem. Do not assert pump-track mileage equals distance-skating preparation. Annual-volume replies include a 2,500-mile aspiration, roughly 1,800 completed toward 2,100, and 500 miles over three months combining mountain biking and skating, followed by a hoped-for 500 in two months. Mixed-sport totals do not identify skating-specific volume. Targets are not required preparation volumes; one unit is inherited ambiguously from question. A mixed-sport annual total is not a skating readiness test. Return to the practical questions to rehearse on the board when conditions allow."},{"title":"Winter training and the limits of transfer","section":"Make the substitute match the available space","url":"guide/winter-and-cross-training.html#recent-late-winter-constraints","topic":"prepare","text":"Just before the 2026 Miami event, a Virginia rider facing icy trails planned rowing-machine sessions and pumping in a garage. Another had used running and cycling because dry trails never coincided with time off work. These accounts explain why the substitutes were chosen; neither supplies a result showing that they replaced long outdoor rides."},{"title":"Switching feet: patterns and practice","section":"One leg, short sets and counting","url":"guide/switch-practice.html#patterns","topic":"prepare","text":"One rider reported 200 miles using only one push leg plus pumping; another also used one leg. Switching was therefore not universal among participants. Switching patterns ranged from 3–5 pushes then occasional mongo when tired, to three per leg, to counting fours, to rotating feet/paddle side every two miles. The rider counting fours chose low mental effort rather than matching every push to music. A successful one-leg account shows that switching was not universal; it does not establish equivalent strain or suitability for another rider."},{"title":"Switching feet: patterns and practice","section":"Break a transition into smaller tasks","url":"guide/switch-practice.html#drills","topic":"prepare","text":"One three-push pattern used the first push to establish balance, the second to confirm stability and the third for force before changing legs. Another progression alternated one right/one left, then two, three and four before restarting. One rider practised switch pushing in a parking lot after pump-track laps; another planned indoor transition practice. These are concrete ways people separated skill work from distance riding. No comparative effectiveness data. Separate low-pressure skill practice from a long distance target. The examples concern balance and familiarity, not a guaranteed efficiency gain."},{"title":"Switching feet: patterns and practice","section":"A 50/50 split is a practice goal","url":"guide/switch-practice.html#targets","topic":"prepare","text":"A rider worked hard on a 50/50 switch split during a 26-km waterfront training ride and planned to see how their body held up afterward. Practice goal, not a universal required split; follow-up in 3618785138385905. The goal here was to learn both sides, not to enforce symmetry every lap. See how riders combined pushing and pumping for the broader choices."},{"title":"Switching feet: patterns and practice","section":"Less shuffling: one pattern and its follow-up","url":"guide/switch-practice.html#forward-feet-followup","topic":"prepare","text":"One rider described keeping both feet facing forward between pushing sets instead of rotating the front foot sideways and back each time. They alternated about five to seven normal pushes with five to seven mongo pushes, placing each foot back behind or in front of the other. They felt this reduced unnecessary movement and shoe wear from repeated shuffling. It is one personal pattern, not a required count or a claim of measured energy savings. The first-timer who tried the placement said it helped. Switching to mongo was still harder at speed, and practising balance on one foot was helping them with that transition. They practised new skills in both regular and switch stance and prioritised form and endurance while treating pumping as something to learn gradually. This follow-up makes the useful test specific: can you place the foot predictably without the extra shuffle? Keep that work within a speed you can control; see how foot placement fits the wider push/pump choice."},{"title":"Switching feet: patterns and practice","section":"Do not infer a deadline for balancing both sides","url":"guide/switch-practice.html#recent-r1-learning-time","topic":"prepare","text":"A newer comment expected it to take a long time to balance right- and left-side pushing. It included neither a practice history nor a measured gap. The switching patterns above are ways to practise, not a timetable for reaching equal ability."},{"title":"Setup, compatibility and mechanical warning signs","section":"Geometry can make a promising setup behave badly","url":"guide/setup-and-maintenance.html#geometry","topic":"equipment","text":"A new Subsonic pumping-board owner reported front and rear wheels turning the same way with Bennett front/Randal rear trucks. Several respondents thought the rear truck was reversed and advised checking its orientation; another noted a deliberately reversed rear near zero degrees may be different. The suggested cause was not confirmed. A rider asked which Riptide Kore durometers/formulas suit torsion tails, low-angle RKP rears and various front trucks. No tested combination was described, leaving those compatibility choices unresolved. Do not substitute model names for a tested setup matrix. The missing compatibility answers are real gaps. Check the exact deck, mounting system and truck with the manufacturer rather than borrowing a durometer from an unrelated build."},{"title":"Setup, compatibility and mechanical warning signs","section":"A setup can change during a ride","url":"guide/setup-and-maintenance.html#changes","topic":"equipment","text":"During a ten-mile morning outing warming from a reported 62 degrees to the mid-80s within an hour, a rider found 75a rear bushings initially loose but functional, then too surfy for pumping and changed to 78a halfway. Single setup/rider, no material or rider weight; not universal temperature-to-durometer table. A Poppy user reported pivot slop and a hanger spherical working upward on rough pavement, making pumping feel dead until stopped and reseated. Another user suggested PTFE tape around the pivot, claiming a few hundred miles of reduced slop, and discussed retaining compound versus weak threadlocker for the spherical. Repair suggestions are unverified; no final repair outcome. Manufacturer guidance needed before advising adhesives or altered bushings. Record the symptom, temperature, surface and loaded configuration. The repair suggestions in the source are not validated instructions for modifying a safety-critical part."},{"title":"Setup, compatibility and mechanical warning signs","section":"Maintenance anecdotes do not establish service intervals","url":"guide/setup-and-maintenance.html#maintenance","topic":"equipment","text":"One rider reports thousands of quiet miles from oiling and wiping bearings without cleaning, while another planned cleaning after a dry-feeling ride. These are different practices without wear inspection or controlled comparison. Manufacturer advice needed for a maintenance instruction. A participant replaced a kingpin shortly before the 2022 Dutch event; no reason, age or failure diagnosis is given. Image unread; do not infer a required replacement interval. Use the equipment checklist to think through compatible spares and a fallback; ask the manufacturer for service procedures and replacement criteria."},{"title":"Setup, compatibility and mechanical warning signs","section":"Separate bearing condition from brand reputation","url":"guide/setup-and-maintenance.html#wet-bearing-history","topic":"equipment","text":"A Seattle rider reported several Zealous bearing sets becoming sluggish after wet use before they had prepared rain bearings. In the same reply, they liked the confidence of large wheels with rain cuts for pumping and carving. Neither observation included a drag or grip measurement. Another rider recalled a Zealous failure during a garage run, while acknowledging that they were not lubricating regularly then. They later reported using the same $7 bearing set for 352 days, including 680 miles in one month, with Riptide Speed WET lubricant. Bearing type, care and use changed together, so the story cannot isolate the brand or lubricant as the cause. Record rain exposure, noise, resistance and the care actually performed. These accounts do not supply a tested service interval or establish that a lubricant is compatible with every bearing; use the component maker’s procedure."},{"title":"Setup, compatibility and mechanical warning signs","section":"Available spares may not be the parts you need","url":"guide/setup-and-maintenance.html#available-spares","topic":"equipment","text":"Near race day, a Supersonic rider sought fresh bearings or lubricant and considered moving from stock bushings to roughly 94a rear and 86a front. A 175-lb rider offered help but had only new 75a/80a bushings or older hard ones without matching pairs. They could offer lubricant instead. The thread records the mismatch, not a completed or proven bushing change. Use the packing checklist to identify the exact parts your setup needs before relying on trackside help. The same exchange contains an unverified solvent-cleaning suggestion; it should not be treated as a maintenance procedure."},{"title":"Setup, compatibility and mechanical warning signs","section":"A lower homemade deck is not a structural test","url":"guide/setup-and-maintenance.html#modified-deck-evidence","topic":"equipment","text":"A rider described converting inexpensive decks to drop-through and found the result low, stable and easy to push. The post supplies no reinforcement details, loaded-clearance checks or durability testing. It therefore documents why the rider liked the modification, but cannot establish how another deck should be cut or whether the result is sound. For a proposed modification, distinguish the desired riding position from the unanswered construction questions. A favourable first impression does not fill those gaps."},{"title":"Setup, compatibility and mechanical warning signs","section":"A wet-weather experiment is not a maintenance specification","url":"guide/setup-and-maintenance.html#recent-wet-bearing-experiment","topic":"equipment","text":"A Seattle rider described combining marine grease with Riptide wet lube to avoid repeatedly replacing Zealous bearings after wet rides. The discussion gives no lifespan after treatment, compatibility check or comparison with another method. It records an attempted solution to a wet-weather problem, not proof of waterproof bearings. Keep the problem and the proposed treatment separate in your notes. Before combining lubricants, check the bearing and lubricant makers’ guidance; this account does not establish a service interval."},{"title":"Setup, compatibility and mechanical warning signs","section":"Keep the missing setup details visible","url":"guide/setup-and-maintenance.html#recent-wheelbase-and-fit-claims","topic":"equipment","text":"One rider said it took five iterations to find an axle-to-axle length that felt right, but gave no final measurement. Another reported that Bee’s Knees fit a Pranayama without specifying the trucks, risers, rider weight or a loaded clearance test. Both are useful starting points for questions; neither supplies a complete build to copy. Record the actual wheelbase and complete configuration when testing a change. A short “it fits” reply does not establish clearance through every movement or with another rider."},{"title":"GPS, battery life and the difference between records","section":"Start-up and battery failures","url":"guide/gps-and-lap-counting.html#devices","topic":"race","text":"A rider missed the first laps of the 2018 Ultra recording by starting before GPS acquisition, then found a Forerunner 935 lasted only 20 hours at the 2019 event. Another described a secondhand Vivoactive 3 failing after almost two years and a 100-mile training weekend. Battery mode, age and settings vary; do not use as current specifications. Participants proposed reduced-sampling UltraTrac modes for longer battery life, with claimed 36–40-hour durations. These are dated user claims, not measured results in this discussion, and entail less frequent location sampling. Verify manufacturer documentation before describing current modes or runtimes. A device lasting 20 hours is not enough evidence for a current product specification. Rehearse your own settings, charging and recording procedure before relying on them."},{"title":"GPS, battery life and the difference between records","section":"An empty live feed is not proof of a stop","url":"guide/gps-and-lap-counting.html#feeds","topic":"race","text":"A volunteer chart-maker reported that the previous year’s lap-timing website stopped uploading data for several hours; a spectator had mistaken the gap for the analyst sleeping. Live chart gaps can reflect upstream data failure rather than rider inactivity. No cause independently verified; do not infer race timing system failure from public upload failure. A live eight-hour update explicitly noted a bug in one rider’s data while projecting another toward 320 miles. A projection or interim leaderboard should not be treated as a final verified result. Screenshot unread; neither result independently verified. Keep provisional projections separate from confirmed results. Do not explain a gap by inventing what a rider was doing."},{"title":"GPS, battery life and the difference between records","section":"Missing recordings do not mean missing effort","url":"guide/gps-and-lap-counting.html#missing","topic":"race","text":"A rider failed to follow their route app and ended up hiking through woods with wet shoes, then pushed hard for 10 km while forgetting to resume Strava. They subsequently felt nauseated, drank little and developed leg cramps; the sequence is reported, not proof of one cause. A rider was reminded of an earlier 199.6-mile result when targeting 200; they said they would not repeat the mistake, but did not explain what caused the shortfall. Do not invent lap-counting or timing explanation. Separate route navigation, recording controls and race scoring in your checklist. Official chip instructions belong to the organiser, not the GPS app."},{"title":"GPS, battery life and the difference between records","section":"Check the measurement behind historical totals","url":"guide/gps-and-lap-counting.html#recent-lap-distance-comparison","topic":"race","text":"An inline participant said the official Miami lap length changed from 1.38 to 1.42 miles in 2025, while their own mapped lap measured 1.41. This made them uncertain how to compare a friend’s earlier result with a new target. These are reported figures, not independently checked course measurements. Before comparing editions, check the scoring distance used for each result rather than substituting an app’s route estimate."},{"title":"Motivation, monotony and changing goals","section":"A target can motivate—and become a stopping point","url":"guide/mental-strategies.html#goals","topic":"race","text":"One first-timer described reaching 100 miles by going slowly and steadily despite little training, while finding it mentally and physically difficult. Another found reaching a preset 200-mile target caused a mental shutdown around hour 23 and planned a higher visible target to keep going. The undertrained success is not evidence that training is unnecessary. A rider describes pre-event butterflies when an important Ultra approaches, a reminder that anticipation can be part of the experience even before riding begins. No clinical diagnosis or advice implied. Use the three-level goal framework to leave room for changing circumstances. A bigger number is not a reason to override a health concern."},{"title":"Motivation, monotony and changing goals","section":"Sunrise, music and shared atmosphere","url":"guide/mental-strategies.html#night","topic":"race","text":"One rider reports a renewed burst of energy at sunrise; other replies prefer sunrise for the feeling of having made it through the night, while one prefers sunset. Psychological experience, not a guarantee that fatigue resolves. One rider accidentally left their music at the motel and was surprisingly content without it; another appreciated the event PA playlist. Personal music is a preference rather than a documented prerequisite. Current event music schedules are not established. Music and dawn mattered differently to different people. Decide which comforts you value, while allowing for an event that feels different from training."},{"title":"Motivation, monotony and changing goals","section":"Give the effort a purpose and keep perspective","url":"guide/mental-strategies.html#meaning","topic":"race","text":"One rider tied family donations to each mile completed, giving distance a fundraising purpose beyond a finish total. Do not republish personal health details or names. Other debrief lessons were to avoid looking at the monitor immediately after crossing the timing loop, protect an MP3 player in a makeshift dry box, and deliberately find humour in the wind rather than let frustration end the attempt. The dry-box construction was not described; the attention lesson remains a personal preference. A useful debrief records motivation alongside practical causes of stopping. Keep, change, test and confirm gives that reflection a place."},{"title":"Motivation, monotony and changing goals","section":"An unexpected century is a story, not a readiness test","url":"guide/mental-strategies.html#first-attempt-context","topic":"race","text":"One rider recalled earning a first 100-mile patch at 248 lb with no training, after expecting fewer than fifty miles. They described an improvised menu and poor preparation the night before. It is a striking individual outcome, not evidence that training or preparation can be skipped. Later retellings of that same century are not separate successful cases. A different first-timer framed Ultra as a fiftieth-birthday family holiday: showing their children an unusual challenge mattered, and the hoped-for century would be a bonus. That gives a concrete alternative to judging the whole trip by one distance number. Use your own definition of a successful first attempt, with room to change it. A surprising result from another person cannot predict your outcome or justify copying their preparation."},{"title":"Motivation, monotony and changing goals","section":"Leave room to change what success means","url":"guide/mental-strategies.html#recent-revised-success","topic":"race","text":"A rider who aimed for 250 miles in their first Ultra finished 215.5 and described being hard on themselves. They later announced a 264-mile target, then revised it before the event and reported 150 miles with an eight-hour nap within the 24 hours. They were pleased with that outcome and smaller side goals. The change in satisfaction is part of the result; the distances and sleep period are personal history, not a recommended schedule. Another participant’s 200/240/250-mile targets came with a practical constraint: blisters one year and fear of repeating them the next had increased time off track, and they wanted to be able to walk around on a later trip. In the revised-goal account, familiar faces, shared jokes and on-course conversations also mattered. A meaningful day can include those experiences alongside a number."},{"title":"Choose a route for the session you need","section":"Scout a particular segment, not just a trail name","url":"guide/practice-routes.html#scout","topic":"prepare","text":"One rider selected an enjoyable section of the St Augustine–Palatka trail for a first skate after having cycled the full route previously. Another identifies the Inglis Dam part of the Florida Greenway as preparation terrain. Specific segment and current surface unspecified. A rider planned to try the Good Neighbor Trail before the organised ride to get a feel for it; another named East Coast Regional Rail Trail as a prospective route. No outcome of the scouting plan was reported. A reconnaissance ride can answer questions about crossings, turnaround points and supply access before a longer rehearsal. This is a planning inference, not a promise that a named route remains open."},{"title":"Choose a route for the session you need","section":"A paved route can still be slow or awkward","url":"guide/practice-routes.html#surface","topic":"prepare","text":"A 2022 visit to the rehabilitated old Seven Mile Bridge found rough, slow pavement and small gaps but no wheel-stopping gaps; the rider did not see a bathroom and found the ground-level paved trail faster and empty. Permission to skate was only inferred from a rollerblade mention. Historical route observations; access/current condition unverified. The same recurring discussion notes difficulty through leaves and that rocks still need attention in wet conditions. Rain-specific wheels do not remove those route hazards in the accounts. Do not infer snow or ice suitability. The historical bridge account separates observed roughness from an unverified assumption about access. Verify permission independently; wet-weather equipment does not remove leaves and stones."},{"title":"Choose a route for the session you need","section":"Short loops and fixed-skate obstacles","url":"guide/practice-routes.html#loops","topic":"prepare","text":"A roller skater described difficulty crossing a Florida Keys cattle grid while holding a fence; this highlights obstacles a board rider can carry over but fixed skates may complicate. Another rider trained on only a half-mile asphalt segment in a Utah park. Exact Keys location uncertain; do not recommend fence-assisted crossing or infer access permission. A Dublin rider described their long-used local cyclepath from East Wall to Sutton as roughly 9 km, with video montages planned. This is historical route context, not current access or surface verification. Use a repeatable section if it helps you rehearse resupply and stops. A route suitable for carrying a board over an obstacle may be unsuitable for inline or quad skates."},{"title":"Choose a route for the session you need","section":"The available loop may be short, busy or shared with cars","url":"guide/practice-routes.html#local-length-and-traffic","topic":"prepare","text":"A rider in Waynesboro, Virginia had only a 2.5-mile local trail and planned occasional trips to meet friends elsewhere; another used a fifteen-mile round trip on Lehigh Trail in Palm Coast. These are different available training spaces, not competing minimum distances for a useful session. A Myakka State Park rider described a freshly paved twelve-mile road as smooth and felt comfortable sharing it with cars under a low speed limit. Their aim was to cover it in under an hour using pushing and pumping. This is one historical experience; it does not verify current skating permission, traffic levels or suitability for another rider. Match the route to the question you are practising: repeatable pace, turns, supplies or duration. Check permission and actual traffic separately from a positive surface report."},{"title":"Choose a route for the session you need","section":"A Miami sightseeing ride can add substantial travel","url":"guide/practice-routes.html#miami-before-the-race","topic":"prepare","text":"For a visitor staying near Homestead, participants described South Beach as roughly ninety minutes away and a pump track as about an hour away without traffic. One described the ocean path as approximately 6.5 miles, preferring early morning before pedestrian crowds. The visitor later reported encountering those crowds. These are historical travel estimates, not live journey times. Krome path was suggested as a more accessible alternative from near the track. One rider preferred it when it was not too sunny; another valued about five miles between a truck stop and the railroad but found the truck noise wearing over several hours. The comparison is convenience, exposure and surroundings—not a verified current route recommendation. Keep a pre-event outing within your travel and rest plan. Check the specific segment, current access and transport rather than assuming every Miami destination is close to the speedway."},{"title":"Choose a route for the session you need","section":"A known trail can change after rain or a repair","url":"guide/practice-routes.html#recent-r1-changing-access","topic":"prepare","text":"After heavy rain, one rider encountered mud washed across a trail beside a river. In the same discussion, another could add miles because the LaChua trailhead water fountain had been repaired. Surface and resupply availability both changed the practical ride without changing the trail’s name. Neither report establishes present conditions. On a familiar daily 10-mile route, a rider described a roughly one-third-mile downhill with speeds up to about 25 mph. A car encounter forced them off the road to roll onto a lawn beside a mailbox. Only the rider’s account is available, so the road layout and fault cannot be established; familiarity clearly did not prevent the reported near miss. For a rehearsal, inspect traffic interactions, stopping options, runoff and functioning refill points as well as the length. Choose a different segment when the conditions no longer fit the session."},{"title":"Choose a route for the session you need","section":"Recent route leads, with the missing details intact","url":"guide/practice-routes.html#recent-r1-recent-leads","topic":"prepare","text":"A UK rider described Titchfield to Stokes Bay as eight miles each way using relatively new cycle path and seafront. They had not ridden it yet that year and were waiting for suitable weather. This is a useful reported length and terrain description, with current access and condition still to check. Other recurring discussions named a Depot Park turnaround on the Hawthorne trail, Tallahassee’s Saint Marks/Capital Circle trails, Kansas City’s Indian Creek Trail and Elks Lake Road in Hattiesburg. The Indian Creek ride was described as “75” without a unit. No safe route length, uninterrupted surface or current access can be inferred from those brief mentions. Participants also planned a whole Virginia Capital Trail ride and a September 2025 group outing. Another exchange praised “Birch” for a skating loop and beach access, without fully identifying the park or giving the loop length. These are leads for local checking rather than current event listings or measured training routes. Dutch pre-race suggestions included Maximapark’s Het Lint, Toolenburgerplas, a Vondelpark loop and a proposed Scheveningen-to-Zandvoort skate. They were suggestions and plans, with no verified route distances or surfaces. Check the route, access and surface before planning a ride."},{"title":"Choose a route for the session you need","section":"A route description is a starting point for scouting","url":"guide/practice-routes.html#recent-route-descriptions","topic":"prepare","text":"Two later reports describe very different outings: a November 2025 visit to Silver Lake Campground with smooth pavement and snake runs, and a 14-mile ride on quiet New Forest lanes in southern England in April 2026. Neither gives a complete route; the campground’s exact location is not specified. These are historical descriptions to investigate, not verified routes or access permissions."},{"title":"Clothing, friction and compression","section":"Why two similar shoes can have very different lifetimes","url":"guide/clothing-and-friction.html#wear","topic":"equipment","text":"A Speedforce wearer reported a grip-tape hole and very thin soles after about 600 miles, mostly goofy while learning skogging. Follow-up clarified frequent footbraking, including five-plus times each way on winding trails. A Prio user instead reported thousands of miles/years with only light braking and rubber deck grip; an HFS user also reported thousands of miles and attributed the difference to a firmer sole than Speedforce. Different models, grip and braking exposure prevent a universal mileage claim; current materials/warranty unverified. A rider reported extending Etnies life by periodically replacing brake soles; the newcomer planned detachable soles on thin barefoot shoes for economy and board feel after watching a tutorial. No outcome of their planned system is posted. Compare the actual model, braking frequency and deck grip before interpreting mileage claims. A proposed modification is not a completed long-ride test."},{"title":"Clothing, friction and compression","section":"Compression is also a clothing and heat choice","url":"guide/clothing-and-friction.html#clothing","topic":"equipment","text":"Compression choices ranged from no compression to calf sleeves left on throughout, light socks, compression underwear with looser outer clothing, and shorts plus knee-high socks. A South Florida rider found full tights trapped too much heat despite liking lighter garments. Fit/thermal preferences, not medical efficacy. Some riders associated compression with more cramps, while another had used sleeves for four Ultras and reconsidered after hearing a podcast anecdote about removing them. They also changed salt strategies, so no causal effect of compression or salt can be isolated. Podcast not listened to; product image unread; do not prescribe supplements or massage as treatment. These reports cannot isolate whether compression changed cramping. Keep the comfort choice separate from a medical claim; see symptoms and medical boundaries."},{"title":"Clothing, friction and compression","section":"Locate the friction before choosing a response","url":"guide/clothing-and-friction.html#friction","topic":"equipment","text":"Suggestions distinguish foot friction (toe socks, thin sock liners), thigh friction (non-cotton synthetic compression shorts, sometimes built into running shorts), and nipple friction that surprised a first-time long-distance participant. Full-length compression pants were a personal cold-weather choice. Suggestions/anecdotes, no comparative efficacy evidence. A rider called anti-chafe cream, lotion or wax non-negotiable after experience; the follow-up asking what product and mileage led to that conclusion had no answer. No product, application method or preventive efficacy established. The practical value is identifying the contact point and the conditions. These experiences do not establish a product or application schedule that will solve every problem."},{"title":"A solo pit and a workable resupply routine","section":"Solo does not mean refusing all help","url":"guide/solo-pit-and-resupply.html#solo","topic":"logistics","text":"Several riders planned solo participation with a box of food and drinks; one says their best distance came without support because they kept skating. That same reply still offered RV/cooler help, showing solo need not mean refusing all assistance. No controlled comparison of crew effectiveness. A rider left food in a box without a lid and birds ate it; a covered storage system would have addressed this specific failure. Closing food containers is editorial application of the incident. A covered box directly addresses the reported bird problem. Arrange help you can actually count on rather than assuming a neighbour will always be available."},{"title":"A solo pit and a workable resupply routine","section":"A bottle grab is different with a paddle","url":"guide/solo-pit-and-resupply.html#handoffs","topic":"logistics","text":"A 2023 reply described large iced water coolers trackside and expected quick bladder refills. Another used disposable bottles, half Tailwind and half plain water, lined along the wall to grab without stepping off. The paddling questioner felt carrying a paddle made that handoff too difficult. Historical availability; practical coordination differs by discipline. A solo pit can be arranged for a bottle grab every lap; one rider planned a canopy to prevent supplies heating. A paddler instead carried hydration because a paddle hindered bottle grabs and stopping sacrificed tailwind momentum; they reported stops every five laps and completing a 50-mile goal. Compare a bottle handoff, a carried reservoir and a deliberate stop in the context of your discipline. Use the crew guide for communication and preparation roles."},{"title":"A solo pit and a workable resupply routine","section":"Do not build the meal plan around assumed electricity","url":"guide/solo-pit-and-resupply.html#power","topic":"logistics","text":"Before the 2022 Dutch Ultra, a respondent recalled kettles being disallowed because a high load might trip the circuit shared with timing; others recalled gas cookers being allowed and offered to share one. The asker had already planned food around not needing hot water. These are recollections, not current electrical or flame permissions. The old kettle and shared-circuit account is a question for the current organiser, not permission to use a stove. Have a meal and charging plan that matches confirmed facilities."},{"title":"A solo pit and a workable resupply routine","section":"If coffee matters to you, plan how it arrives","url":"guide/solo-pit-and-resupply.html#recent-r1-coffee","topic":"logistics","text":"Riders described coffee as scarce enough at their past event to bring their own. One suggested a coffee machine; another bought a cold brewer to prepare a batch in advance. The latter believed it was less acidic, but the discussion did not test that claim. The useful logistical difference is between needing an appliance at the track and arriving with a drink already prepared. Check the current supply and appliance permissions. A reported wall outlet does not settle whether your coffee equipment is permitted or practical."},{"title":"Health questions that need individual advice","section":"Heat and fluid problems are not interchangeable","url":"guide/health-and-warning-signs.html#heat","topic":"after","text":"CDC advises stopping activity and moving somewhere cool if you feel faint or weak in the heat. Its Yellow Book warns that excessive forced drinking during prolonged exercise can contribute to exercise-associated hyponatremia. Early symptoms can overlap with heat illness; confusion or collapse is a reason for urgent medical help, not an experiment with a community salt or water recipe."},{"title":"Health questions that need individual advice","section":"Do not copy a race-day painkiller routine","url":"guide/health-and-warning-signs.html#medicines","topic":"after","text":"NIDDK warns that NSAIDs, including ibuprofen and naproxen, can cause acute kidney injury when someone is dehydrated or has low blood pressure. Discuss medicines and an appropriate plan with your clinician or pharmacist. Someone else’s use of a medicine is not evidence of safety for you."},{"title":"Health questions that need individual advice","section":"Warning signs may appear after the finish","url":"guide/health-and-warning-signs.html#after","topic":"after","text":"CDC lists muscle pain or cramps worse than expected, tea- or cola-coloured urine, and unusual weakness or exercise intolerance as possible rhabdomyolysis symptoms. They may appear hours or days after exertion. Seek medical care immediately for these symptoms; symptoms alone cannot confirm the diagnosis, which requires clinical testing."},{"title":"Health questions that need individual advice","section":"The journey home needs actual rest","url":"guide/health-and-warning-signs.html#driving","topic":"after","text":"NHTSA says adequate sleep is the central protection against drowsy driving. Coffee or energy drinks alone are insufficient when someone is seriously sleep-deprived; brief involuntary sleeps may still occur. Arrange a rested driver or recovery accommodation before the event. See travel and recovery arrangements for the practical choices raised by participants. A distance goal does not remove the need for a safe way home."},{"title":"Health questions that need individual advice","section":"Opposite knee-recovery stories are not interchangeable","url":"guide/health-and-warning-signs.html#different-knee-injuries","topic":"after","text":"One rider with torn menisci felt that distance skating without enough time between sessions prolonged their recovery. Another, who described a tibial-plateau fracture but an intact meniscus, credited skating with helping them return. These are different injuries, care histories and personal interpretations—not a comparison of rehabilitation methods. The second rider also repeated their return story elsewhere, including using paddling and pumping when unable to push. Repetition by the same person adds detail, not independent confirmation. Whether an activity is appropriate for someone else’s injury needs an individual assessment."},{"title":"Health questions that need individual advice","section":"A first-century knee question remained unanswered","url":"guide/health-and-warning-signs.html#recent-r1-first-century","topic":"after","text":"After a first 100-mile skate, a rider reported unexpectedly severe knee-joint pain and asked how to condition their knees beyond regular skating. Replies listed swimming, yoga, squats, lunges, kettlebells, massage and other activities. None described a diagnosis, an individual assessment or a later recovery outcome. This exchange identifies a problem worth assessing, but cannot tell the rider which exercise is appropriate or whether to continue skating. Keep it separate from general examples of alternative training."},{"title":"Formats, official rules and timing","section":"Different disciplines create different practical constraints","url":"guide/event-formats-and-rules.html#formats","topic":"logistics","text":"A paddler seeking a relay place had no match after several weeks; a reply directed them to SUPSKATE USA contacts. Finding a team was not automatic, and no final placement was reported. Confirm discipline, solo or relay entry, team formation, awards and who is permitted on course. Do not infer current eligibility from an old participation story."},{"title":"Formats, official rules and timing","section":"A dated check of the Miami 2027 rules","url":"guide/event-formats-and-rules.html#miami","topic":"logistics","text":"The official Miami 2027 page requires a helmet on track, counts completed full laps only, and limits crew assistance to pit road. Individuals use the supplied ankle chip and wear a bib on their back; the page warns against taping the chip or placing it above the ankle. Teams use a baton with a chip and are told to carry it low across the timing line. These are edition-specific rules, not a substitute for the latest briefing."},{"title":"Formats, official rules and timing","section":"Keep unresolved logistics on a confirmation list","url":"guide/event-formats-and-rules.html#confirm","topic":"logistics","text":"Check arrival and departure access, camping nights, crew registration, resupply locations, electricity and cooking, first-aid contact, passing and merging, and the final-lap cutoff. Participants have different recollections about several of these arrangements. Use travel planning and crew preparation to turn them into concrete arrangements."},{"title":"Formats, official rules and timing","section":"Finding a warm-up event still requires checking the format","url":"guide/event-formats-and-rules.html#recent-r1-event-leads","topic":"logistics","text":"A new skater in southeast England asked for UK or overseas events to use before an Ultra and received no reported answer. A later event-collection discussion named the Chief Ladiga Silver Comet Skate Challenge, Broad Street Bomb, Broadway Bomb and pump-track activity at Chicago’s Big Marsh Park. Those are different kinds of outing, and no complete current calendar or next Big Marsh date was established. A respondent pointed to Beyond the Board’s group map and a prior-year personal events calendar, explicitly saying the details needed updating. Their proposed Poker Push on the Little Miami Scenic Trail was tentative. Treat these as leads to check with the organisers, rather than confirmed dates or participation recommendations. Before treating an outing as a rehearsal, establish its distance, terrain, traffic arrangements, support and entry requirements. A social push, pump-track session and long-distance event answer different preparation questions."},{"title":"Formats, official rules and timing","section":"An informal test lap is not automatic track access","url":"guide/event-formats-and-rules.html#recent-r1-dutch-practice","topic":"logistics","text":"A Dutch Ultra contributor described a Friday dinner followed by meeting at the track for test laps. They later added an explicit condition: if cyclists were still training, skaters had to wait and must not enter early. The social plan did not supply official permission or a confirmed access time. Confirm when the track becomes available, even if other participants are planning to meet there. Keep a social invitation separate from permission to enter the course."},{"title":"Formats, official rules and timing","section":"Check the edition throughout the registration page","url":"guide/event-formats-and-rules.html#recent-registration-edition","topic":"logistics","text":"A 30 November 2025 reminder said Miami’s early-bird price would rise by $50 the next day. A reader then found a 2025 heading on the registration page; another said the correct edition appeared farther down. The useful lesson is the mismatch, not the old fee: check that the event date, entry category and checkout refer to the edition you intend to enter. Confirm the current price and deadline with the organiser."}];
